{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And He called, etc.</b> Here He informed that these are the things spoken of in the verse ״בְּרֵאשִׁית״ etc., and what He called them by the name 'heavens' in the first verse means: one creation that He would eventually call 'heavens,' and HaShem never called it by this name until the second day." "<b>And according to</b> our Rabbis, who said (Genesis Rabbah ch. 4) that the meaning of 'heavens' (שָׁמַיִם) is 'lift water' (שָׂא מַיִם), the verse intends along this line: <b>And HaShem called the firmament</b> and said to it, ״Lift water״ (שָׂא מַיִם), so that it would become air for the space of the created beings, so that the created beings would be between heaven and earth; and without this there would be no place for the inhabitants of the world until He called to the firmament to lift the waters that were resting upon it. And He said to the lower waters that they should gather to one place on the third day, and the dry land would appear — and behold, a place for the inhabitants of the world. And there is no contradiction to this from the words of our Rabbis, who said that the upper waters stand by miracle, for even though the heavens bear them, despite this, in order to arrange the structure and ordering of the heavens, they [the Rabbis] perceived that they stand by miracle, as we wrote in connection with the verse ״וַיַּעַשׂ״."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T18:54:15.271589674Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}